## How It Works

```
┌─────────┐                          ┌─────────┐
│ Client  │                          │ Server  │
└─────────┘                          └─────────┘
     │  POST /register (name+email+password)   │
     │ ──────────────────────────────────────► │  Hash password, save user
     │  { message: "Registered!" }             │
     │ ◄────────────────────────────────────── │
     │                                         │
     │  POST /login (email+password)           │
     │ ──────────────────────────────────────► │  Verify hash, sign JWT
     │  { token: "eyJhbG..." }                 │
     │ ◄────────────────────────────────────── │
     │                                         │
     │  GET /profile                           │
     │  Authorization: Bearer eyJhbG...        │
     │ ──────────────────────────────────────► │  Verify token signature
     │  { user: { id, email } }                │  Decode & return data
     │ ◄────────────────────────────────────── │
```

### JWT Structure

```
eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9   ← Header (algorithm)
.eyJpZCI6MSwiZW1haWwiOiJ0ZXN0QHRlc3QuY29tIn0  ← Payload (your data)
.abc123signature                         ← Signature (tamper-proof)
```

---

## Dependencies

| Package | Version | Purpose |
|---------|---------|---------|
| `express` | ^4.x | HTTP server and routing |
| `jsonwebtoken` | ^9.x | JWT signing and verification |
| `bcryptjs` | ^2.x | Password hashing |
| `dotenv` | ^16.x | Environment variable loading |

---

> **Note on Data Persistence:** This project uses an in-memory array to store users. All data is lost when the server restarts. A real application would use a database such as MongoDB or PostgreSQL.